## Working rules (hard rules)

1. **One layer at a time.** Build only the layer the student asked for. Do not start the
   next layer, do not "improve" earlier layers unless asked. Stop when it is done.
2. **Plan first.** Before writing code, state in 3–6 lines what you will create/change.
3. **Do not change public interfaces** (names, signatures, return types in
   `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`) without asking. Every agent must stay interchangeable.
4. **Tests are part of the layer.** Write tests with the code. Run them. Report the real
   output. Never claim something passes without running it. Never delete or weaken a test
   to make it pass.
5. **Be honest about uncertainty.** If a requirement is unclear, ask one short question or
   state your assumption in the plan. Do not invent results or numbers.
6. **No fake data.** Experiment results must come from actually running the code.
   Never hardcode numbers into graphs or reports.
7. **Determinism.** Anything random takes a seed (`random.Random(seed)`). No use of the
   global `random` state. Same seed => same result.
8. **Keep the board safe.** `choose_move(board)` must leave `board` exactly as it found it
   (search with `drop`/`undo`). Add a test for this.
9. **Code style:** type hints on public functions, a docstring on every public function and
   class, short functions (aim under ~40 lines), constants in `config.py`, no global mutable
   state, no wildcard imports. Comments explain _why_, not just _what_.
10. **No new dependencies, no network calls, no files outside the project folder.**

